Let $X_{1}$ and $X_{2}$ be the number of heads for the first coin and the second coin respectively. Since these events are independent we have,
\begin{equation}\text{Var}(X)=\text{Var}(X_{1})+\text{Var}(X_{2})\end{equation}
So now I compute the separate variances,
\begin{equation}\text{Var}(X_{1})=\mathbb{E}[X_{1}^{2}]-(\mathbb{E}[X_{1}])^{2}\end{equation}
\begin{equation}\text{Var}(X_{1})=5^{2}\cdot\frac{1}{4}-(5\cdot\frac{1}{4})^{2}\end{equation}
\begin{equation}\text{Var}(X_{1})=\frac{25}{4}-\frac{25}{16}=\frac{75}{16}\end{equation}
and for $X_{2}$,
\begin{equation}\text{Var}(X_{2})=\mathbb{E}[X_{2}^{2}]-(\mathbb{E}[X_{2}])^{2}\end{equation}
\begin{equation}\text{Var}(X_{2})=5^{2}\cdot\frac{3}{4}-(5\cdot\frac{3}{4})^{2}\end{equation}
\begin{equation}\text{Var}(X_{2})=\frac{75}{4}-\frac{225}{16}=\frac{75}{16}\end{equation}
So now to conclude I compute,
\begin{equation}\text{Var}(X)=\frac{75}{16}+\frac{75}{16}=\frac{75}{8}\end{equation}
